    {
      "actual_eos_at_stop": true,
      "answer_segment": "To determine the number of chickens on Farmer Brown's farm, we can set up a system of equations based on the information given. Let's define the variables first:\n\n- Let \\( c \\) be the number of chickens.\n- Let \\( k \\) be the number of cows.\n\nWe know two things:\n1. The total number of animals is 20.\n2. The total number of legs is 70.\n\nFrom the first piece of information, we can write the equation:\n\\[ c + k = 20 \\]\n\nFrom the second piece of information, we know that each chicken has 2 legs and each cow has 4 legs. Therefore, we can write the equation for the total number of legs as:\n\\[ 2c + 4k = 70 \\]\n\nWe now have a system of two equations:\n1. \\( c + k = 20 \\)\n2. \\( 2c + 4k = 70 \\)\n\nTo solve this system, we can start by simplifying the second equation. We can divide every term in the second equation by 2:\n\\[ c + 2k = 35 \\]\n\nNow we have the simplified system:\n1. \\( c + k = 20 \\)\n2. \\( c + 2k = 35 \\)\n\nNext, we can subtract the first equation from the second equation to eliminate \\( c \\):\n\\[ (c + 2k) - (c + k) = 35 - 20 \\]\n\\[ c + 2k - c - k = 15 \\]\n\\[ k = 15 \\]\n\nSo, there are 15 cows. Now we can substitute \\( k = 15 \\) back into the first equation to find \\( c \\):\n\\[ c + 15 = 20 \\]\n\\[ c = 20 - 15 \\]\n\\[ c = 5 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of chickens is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
